  the tab mid-request is a rounding error, not a defect.

  Next question: [1mwhere do the events go?[0m This is the one real dependency decision in the design, and it's the expensive
  one to change later — it determines the event format, the failure handling, and whether this repo gains a third-party
  script.

  - [1mA third-party analytics service[0m (PostHog, Plausible, Amplitude, GA4, etc.) — you get dashboards, retention, and
    session handling for free, and the send-and-retry logic is the vendor's problem. Cost: a third-party script on your
    login page, a vendor account, and a data-processor relationship for what is personal data. Changing vendors later
    means re-instrumenting, though if we put a thin wrapper in front of it that's a contained change.
  - [1mYour own endpoint[0m (POST the event to an API you control, alongside the existing [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m auth host) — no
    third party, no vendor script, full control over retention and format. Cost: you need that endpoint to exist and
    someone has to build the query/dashboard side, which is where "just log it" quietly becomes a data project. Cheapest
    to swap [3mto[0m later, most expensive to get value from now.
  - [1mDefer the destination; build the seam[0m — emit events through a small [38;5;153mtrackEvent[39m module whose only implementation for
    now writes to the console, with the real transport dropped in when you've picked one. Cost: no data until you finish
    the decision. Buys: the app-side work is done, correct, and testable, and the choice above stops blocking it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third, then the first. This repo currently has zero dependencies and no backend; picking a
  vendor today is the heaviest possible first move for a stub [38;5;153mlogin[39m that doesn't even make a network call yet. Build the
  seam now — it's a couple of small, well-bounded units — and the vendor becomes a contained change behind it rather
  than something threaded through your login path. If you already have an analytics vendor in the real system this stub
  represents, say so and we go straight to option one.
